\f1\fs22 \'93Indeed, CEO,\'94 Gerry confirmed, keeping it toneless.\
\'93He remains a beacon, a shining example of Airborne ideals. The best of us.\'94\
\'93Indeed,\'94 Gerry agreed, letting the revisionist history pass.\
Abruptly, Dunhilton stood, as if cued by a director off-camera. \'93I need a drink.\'94 He stalked to a cabinet, selected two expensive tumblers and a mostly full bottle of amber liquid.\
\'93Scotch?\'94 he offered.\
Gerry winced internally. \'93It\'92s midday, CEO, so yes, thank you.\'94 He knew what was coming.\
Dunhilton poured, splashed two fingers into each glass and returned. \'93Tasmanian Scotch, nothing better.\'94 He raised his glass for a toast, clinked it against Gerry\'92s untouched one and took a long swallow.\
As a trade delegation leader, Dunhilton had once sued and technically won the right to call Ulyssian spirits \'93Scotch\'94. Later, when it was revealed the naming asset was not contractually owned by Ulyssia, but by a Ulyssian citizen, one Piotr Dunhilton, his star rose yet higher. He\'92d been trading on it ever since.\
This day, bringing out the bottle was an obvious crutch: CEO-as-conquistador, leaning on the legend. The fact that it tasted like concentrated wallaby piss only added to its exclusivity.\
Dunhilton set his glass down with unnecessary volume. \'93Summarise it for me.\'94\
Gerry opened his own copy of the folder, watching Dunhilton gaze at the cover: 
\f2\i HIBReLabs
\f1\i0 .\
\'93Hardy Industries Biological Research Labs,\'94 Gerry baited. \'93You want to know if they actually existed?\'94\
Dunhilton nodded, suddenly wary.\
\'93We have credible evidence they did,\'94 Gerry said. \'93Enough to locate several old sites.\'94 He flipped to a map. \'93HIBReLabs built at least seven major installations in Ulyssia and the Pacific territories, back during our biological arms race with the Sino-sympathetic states.\'94\
Dunhilton started to speak; Gerry stilled him with a finger. \'93But just as they made real breakthroughs, Jamison Hardy shut everything down. Sealed them all. Erased any trace of them.\'94\
Dunhilton\'92s eyebrows climbed. \'93Staff?\'94\
\'93In at least one, he poured concrete down the lift wells while staff were hundreds of metres down in the subterranean installation.\'94 Gerry tapped two file photos. \'93Dr Hamish Hermann and Dr Janine Anstey, entombed alive, along with their entire staff. Even the cleaners.\'94\
\'93What the tax were they working on?\'94 Whatever it was, Dunhilton wanted it. \'93Something we could fight the Elvi with?\'94\
Gerry nodded. \'93Hamish and Anstey engineered a lentiviral vector with programmable geneti
\f3 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 c
\f1 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth0  specificity. They called it TMinus. Imagine taking the DNA profile for an individual. Or, with a few lines of code, a whole phenotype\'97melanin density, specific metabolic pathways, geographic lineage... and encoding it as the access key.\'94\
Dunhilton\'92s gaze remained blank.\
\'93
\f3 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The vector can enter someone without the nominated profile and pass unnoticed.
\f1 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth0  You could infect the world, and only your target dies. No collateral damage. No ruined infrastructure. Just a surgical genetic strike.\'94\
Dunhilton took this in, then his brain worked overtime. \'93So that\'92s how we deal with the Elvi.\'94\
Gerry nodded. \'93They\'92re clones; one base genotype. Distribute by air, water, food or a strategic \'91bacon fat\'92 spill\'97done.\'94\
Dunhilton almost smiled, then caught his reflection. \'93So we just raid an old lab for the stuff and it\'92s solved?\'94\
Gerry nodded. \'93The only way this is controlled is if I have direct command. PAPER oversight for intelligence and logistics. TRAMPS for operations.\'94\
Dunhilton hesitated, whirring internally. \'93Done. It\'92s yours.\'94\
Gerry finally sipped his whisky and said nothing.
